From: Shreyan Dey <shreyan@example.com>
Subject: [PATCH] net: wireless: esp32-ng: run the Wi-Fi blob inside Linux

The ESP32-S3's Wi-Fi is a closed-source binary blob. This tree has always
driven it the way Espressif intends: the blob runs on core 0 as ESP-IDF
firmware, and Linux talks to it over shared memory through the esp-hosted
transport in esp_shmem.c. That works, and it costs core 0 entirely -- the
core is the radio, and nothing else runs there.

This patch takes the other road, the one GrieferPig/esp32-s31-linux took:
link the blob's own libraries into this kernel and host them here. Wi-Fi
then runs on the Linux cores, core 0 is freed, and the transport between
the two halves disappears because there are no longer two halves.

WHAT THE BLOB ACTUALLY NEEDS

The blob is not a driver. It is a set of libraries -- libnet80211.a,
libpp.a, libcore.a, libwapi.a, libespnow.a, libphy.a -- that expect to be
running under ESP-IDF, and that reach out to their host through a table of
function pointers. That table is the OS interface, struct osi_funcs, and
the ROM keeps the pointer to it in a fixed slot: g_osi_funcs_p, at
0x3fcef940 in internal SRAM 1 Block 8. The blob does not take the table as
an argument; it reads that address. Installing the interface is therefore
one writel() to a fixed address, which is what esp_blob.c does at probe.

The address is in the device tree rather than compiled in, so a different
part number or a ROM revision that moves the slot needs a device-tree edit
and not a rebuild.

Measured, not guessed, the station-only archive set leaves twenty-one
symbols undefined once the ROM linker scripts are accounted for. Thirteen
are the mesh entry points, which libmesh.a defines and which this port
stubs because mesh is not offered here; eight are supplied by the two
support files. Everything else the blob reaches for is a ROM address, and
those are bound by name in arch/xtensa/kernel/vmlinux.lds.S -- the ROM's
own linker script (esp32s3.rom.ld) describes a different image's memory
layout and is not linked into this kernel.

THE THREE POWER HOOKS ARE NOT DECORATION

The OS interface is not only malloc and delay. Three of its entries --
_set_intr, _set_isr and the _ints_on/_ints_off pair -- are how the blob
installs its interrupt handlers, and the blob will not start without them.
They are wired to real Linux IRQs through the interrupt matrix: _set_intr
is handed a (matrix row, core interrupt) pair, which is exactly the two
cells the esp32-intc domain takes, so one irq_create_fwspec_mapping() and
one request_irq() put the blob's handler on the hardware. The stub logs
what it was given, which is what makes a board run show whether the blob
asked for the sources the TRM says it should.

THE EVENT PATH

The blob raises Wi-Fi events through the OS interface's _event_post, and
the driver's upper half is what turns them into cfg80211 state. In the IDF
build the firmware's wifi_event_handler() translates each IDF event into
the driver's wire format and hands it to send_command_event(); here there
is no firmware in between, so esp_wifi_blob_event_post() does that
translation itself, reading the same structures the firmware read and
producing the same wire events. The encoding is the firmware's rather than
a new one, because the host half already parses it.

THE TRANSPORT

There is none, and that is the point. esp_shmem.c exists because the blob
was on the other core; with the blob in this kernel there is nothing to
transport. The node hangs off &{/} rather than &ipc for the same reason.

WHAT IS NOT DONE YET

  - the blob is linked, probed and given a populated init config, but not
    yet started. Bringing it up is the next step, and the one that tells us
    whether the ROM addresses and the OS interface are right.
  - the association path still depends on the blob delivering the
    association response as a management frame; if it arrives as an event
    instead, WIFI_EVENT_STA_CONNECTED needs the same treatment
    EVENT_ASSOC_RX gets in the firmware.

diff --git a/arch/xtensa/Makefile b/arch/xtensa/Makefile
index 1111111111111..2222222222222 100644
--- a/arch/xtensa/Makefile
+++ b/arch/xtensa/Makefile
@@ -68,6 +68,88 @@
 
 libs-y		+= arch/xtensa/lib/
 
+# The closed-source Wi-Fi libraries, linked straight into vmlinux when the
+# blob port is on. They are archives, not objects, so they go through libs-y
+# and the kernel's own link rule puts them inside --start-group/--end-group
+# (scripts/link-vmlinux.sh), which is what lets them reference each other in
+# any order.
+#
+# The path is relative to the kernel tree, which Buildroot unpacks at
+# build/linux-7.2.4 under its O= directory. esp-hosted sits beside buildroot
+# in the build driver, so ../../../esp-hosted reaches it: three levels up
+# from build/linux-7.2.4 to the driver root. The blob-libs stage in
+# container-build.sh puts the IDF submodule -- and therefore these archives
+# -- on disk before this stage runs, because the kernel links in base-rootfs,
+# which is earlier than firmware.
+#
+# Only the five station archives plus libphy.a. libmesh.a, libsmartconfig.a
+# and the rest are deliberately absent: libmesh.a is what supplies the
+# thirteen mesh-only symbols the station set leaves undefined, and dropping
+# it is what makes the station-only link close.
+libs-$(CONFIG_ESP32_WIFI_BLOB) += $(srctree)/../../../esp-hosted/esp_hosted_ng/esp/esp_driver/esp-idf/components/esp_wifi/lib/esp32s3/libnet80211.a
+libs-$(CONFIG_ESP32_WIFI_BLOB) += $(srctree)/../../../esp-hosted/esp_hosted_ng/esp/esp_driver/esp-idf/components/esp_wifi/lib/esp32s3/libpp.a
+libs-$(CONFIG_ESP32_WIFI_BLOB) += $(srctree)/../../../esp-hosted/esp_hosted_ng/esp/esp_driver/esp-idf/components/esp_wifi/lib/esp32s3/libcore.a
+libs-$(CONFIG_ESP32_WIFI_BLOB) += $(srctree)/../../../esp-hosted/esp_hosted_ng/esp/esp_driver/esp-idf/components/esp_wifi/lib/esp32s3/libwapi.a
+libs-$(CONFIG_ESP32_WIFI_BLOB) += $(srctree)/../../../esp-hosted/esp_hosted_ng/esp/esp_driver/esp-idf/components/esp_wifi/lib/esp32s3/libespnow.a
+libs-$(CONFIG_ESP32_WIFI_BLOB) += $(srctree)/../../../esp-hosted/esp_hosted_ng/esp/esp_driver/esp-idf/components/esp_phy/lib/esp32s3/libphy.a
+#
+# libcoexist.a is not part of the radio blob. It is the IDF component that
+# IDF's own wifi_init.c calls before esp_wifi_init() -- coex_pre_init() and
+# esp_coex_adapter_register() -- and that this port therefore has to call
+# itself. esp_blob.c calls both from its probe. Without the archive the link
+# fails on undefined symbols, which is the intended failure mode: the
+# alternative is a probe that links and then faults inside the blob.
+#
+# The archive is a thin wrapper over the mask ROM: coex_env, coex_pti_tab,
+# coex_schm_init and the hardware timer helpers are defined inside it, but
+# nearly everything else it references is a fixed ROM address. Those names
+# are bound in the vmlinux.lds.S hunk below, the same way the Wi-Fi ROM data
+# already is.
+#
+# libesp_event.a is deliberately NOT linked. It was, for
+# esp_event_loop_create_default(), and it does not work: that function lives
+# in default_event_loop.c.obj, which needs esp_event_loop_create, which
+# lives in esp_event.c.obj, which needs FreeRTOS -- xQueueGenericCreate,
+# xTaskCreatePinnedToCore, vTaskDelete, xQueueReceive and nine more. This
+# port has no FreeRTOS. And the call is not needed anyway: the blob posts
+# through osi->_event_post, the OS interface at offset 180 of struct
+# wifi_osi_funcs, not through the IDF event loop. Nothing in the blob's
+# init path touches esp_event_loop_*. See
+# experiments/mmu-poc/fork/COEX-LINK-ANALYSIS.md.
+libs-$(CONFIG_ESP32_WIFI_BLOB) += $(srctree)/../../../esp-hosted/esp_hosted_ng/esp/esp_driver/esp-idf/components/esp_coex/lib/esp32s3/libcoexist.a
+#
+# libesp_phy.a is deliberately NOT linked, though it does define
+# coexist_printf.
+#
+# libcoexist.a calls coexist_printf from coexist_api.o, coexist_core.o,
+# coexist_param.o and coexist_scheme.o, and does not define it. It is not
+# in the ROM either -- esp32s3.rom.ld has no such name. lib_printf.c.obj
+# in libesp_phy.a (the IDF esp_phy component's own build product, under
+# network_adapter/build/esp-idf/esp_phy/, 102868 bytes) does define it --
+# but so does esp_blob_phy.c, and linking the archive fails the link:
+#
+#   ld: libesp_phy.a(lib_printf.c.obj): in function `phy_printf':
+#   components/esp_phy/src/lib_printf.c:46: multiple definition of
+#   `phy_printf'; esp_blob_phy.o:esp_blob_phy.c:(.text+0x250): first
+#   defined here
+#
+# and the same for rtc_printf. lib_printf.c.obj defines the whole family
+# -- phy_printf, rtc_printf, wpa_printf, wpa2_printf, wps_printf,
+# pp_printf, sc_printf, core_printf, net80211_printf, wapi_printf,
+# mesh_printf and coexist_printf -- as global symbols in ONE object file,
+# and the linker pulls a whole member at a time. Two of the twelve are
+# already defined in esp_blob_phy.c, so the member cannot be pulled.
+#
+# Nor is anything lost by leaving it out. All twelve shims in that member
+# are identical trampolines into a local clone, lib_printf$constprop$0,
+# which formats into a 79-byte stack buffer and returns without writing
+# the buffer anywhere -- there is no sink at all, so the archive's
+# coexist_printf logs nothing. esp_blob_phy.c's version goes to pr_info.
+#
+# This is NOT the same file as libphy.a above. libphy.a is the radio
+# blob's PHY (components/esp_phy/lib/esp32s3/, 223754 bytes, with
+# register_chipv7_phy and the rest) and is still linked.
+#
 boot		:= arch/xtensa/boot
 
 all Image zImage uImage xipImage: vmlinux
diff --git a/arch/xtensa/kernel/vmlinux.lds.S b/arch/xtensa/kernel/vmlinux.lds.S
index 1111111111111..2222222222222 100644
--- a/arch/xtensa/kernel/vmlinux.lds.S
+++ b/arch/xtensa/kernel/vmlinux.lds.S
@@ -20,6 +20,37 @@
 #include <asm/page.h>
 #include <asm/thread_info.h>
 
+/*
+ * The Wi-Fi blob archives (libcoexist.a and the esp_wifi objects) carry
+ * their initialised data in sections named .dram1.1 through .dram1.7.
+ * Nothing in this tree names those sections, so the linker treats them as
+ * orphans and places them after the last output section in the RAM region.
+ *
+ * That placement is silently wrong under CONFIG_XIP_KERNEL. _edata ends the
+ * range head.S copies from flash to RAM (_sdata.._edata), so an orphan past
+ * it is never initialised: the flash image holds the right bytes, RAM holds
+ * whatever the PSRAM powered up with, and nothing reports a problem.
+ *
+ * coex_env is the one that bit. coex_core_pre_init reads coex_env[0], and a
+ * non-zero value there means "the semaphore already exists", so it skips
+ * _semphr_create; coex_core_lock then blocks forever on a handle that was
+ * never created. On this board coex_env[0] read 0x5c2a132a out of PSRAM
+ * while the flash image holds 0x00000000.
+ *
+ * DATA_MAIN is what DATA_DATA expands into the .data output section, which
+ * under XIP is the one between _sdata and _edata. Appending the .dram1
+ * names to it puts them in that section, so they are copied like any other
+ * initialised data. They are PROGBITS with real contents, which is exactly
+ * what .data is for.
+ *
+ * asm-generic/vmlinux.lds.h defines DATA_MAIN unconditionally, so this
+ * redefinition has to come after the include below. It is arch-local:
+ * no other architecture sees it.
+ */
+#define DATA_MAIN .data .data.[0-9a-zA-Z_]* .data.rel.* .data..L* \
+	.data..compoundliteral* .data.$__unnamed_* .data.$L* \
+	.dram1.1 .dram1.2 .dram1.3 .dram1.4 .dram1.5 .dram1.6 .dram1.7
+
 #include <asm/core.h>
 #include <asm/vectors.h>
 
@@ -79,6 +79,142 @@
 
 SECTIONS
 {
+  /*
+   * The Wi-Fi blob port calls into the ESP32-S3 mask ROM, and the ROM's own
+   * linker script (esp32s3.rom.ld, from ESP-IDF) is what names those entry
+   * points. That script is not linked into this kernel -- it describes a
+   * different image's memory layout -- so the names the blob archives
+   * reference are bound here instead.
+   *
+   * These are absolute addresses, not sections: nothing is placed, and the
+   * ROM is already mapped at boot. Each value is copied from
+   * esp32s3.rom.ld, and the object files that reference them are pulled in
+   * by the archives libs-y adds when CONFIG_ESP32_WIFI_BLOB is on.
+   *
+   * ets_delay_us is the one exception: esp32s3.rom.ld names it at
+   * 0x40000600, but esp32-wifi-blob-support.c defines it against the
+   * kernel's own udelay, so it is deliberately not bound here. Binding it
+   * would collide with that definition.
+   */
+/* ROM text: functions the blob calls and the ROM linker scripts
+ * export. arch/xtensa does not link esp32s3.rom.ld, so without these
+ * the final link has no address to resolve them against.
+ */
+	__divdf3 = 0x40002250;
+	__divdi3 = 0x4000225c;
+	__divsf3 = 0x40002274;
+	__extendsfdf2 = 0x400022a4;
+	__fixdfsi = 0x400022d4;
+	__floatsidf = 0x40002334;
+	__floatundisf = 0x40002358;
+	__moddi3 = 0x400023f4;
+	__popcountsi2 = 0x400024d8;
+	__subdf3 = 0x400024fc;
+	__udivdi3 = 0x40002544;
+	__umoddi3 = 0x40002574;
+	esp_net80211_rom_version_get = 0x400059b8;
+	esp_pp_rom_version_get = 0x40005250;
+	ets_backup_dma_copy = 0x40000624;
+	printf = 0x400015d8;
+	rom_read_hw_noisefloor = 0x40005ec8;
+	roundup2 = 0x40005b14;
+	phy_get_romfuncs = 0x40005c58;
+	rom_phy_param_addr = 0x40006324;
+	rom_rx_gain_force = 0x4000642c;
+	strcpy = 0x40001218;
+	strncmp = 0x4000123c;
+	strncpy = 0x40001224;
+	/* ROM data: the Wi-Fi driver's globals, in internal SRAM 1
+	 * Block 8 at 0x3fcef8xx, outside Linux's RAM (which ends at
+	 * 0x3e000000). The ROM's own Wi-Fi code owns these; the blob
+	 * libraries reach them by absolute address, and so do we.
+	 */
+	BasicOFDMSched_ptr = 0x3fcef8f0;
+	ap_no_lr_ptr = 0x3fcef900;
+	g_chm = 0x3fcef850;
+	g_config_func = 0x3fcef8c0;
+	g_eb_list_desc_ptr = 0x3fcef92c;
+	g_esp_mesh_quick_funcs_ptr = 0x3fcef8dc;
+	g_hmac_cnt_ptr = 0x3fcef848;
+	g_ic_ptr = 0x3fcef84c;
+	g_intr_lock_mux = 0x3fcef920;
+	g_lmac_cnt_ptr = 0x3fcef934;
+	g_mac_sleep_en_ptr = 0x3fcef8d4;
+	g_mesh_init_ps_type_ptr = 0x3fcef8c8;
+	g_mesh_is_root_ptr = 0x3fcef8d0;
+	g_mesh_is_started_ptr = 0x3fcef8c4;
+	g_mesh_topology_ptr = 0x3fcef8cc;
+	g_net80211_tx_func = 0x3fcef8bc;
+	g_osi_funcs_p = 0x3fcef940;
+	g_per_conn_trc = 0x3fcef860;
+	g_pm_cfg_ptr = 0x3fcef8e0;
+	g_pm_cnt_ptr = 0x3fcef8e8;
+	g_pm_ptr = 0x3fcef8e4;
+	g_scan = 0x3fcef854;
+	g_timer_func = 0x3fcef8b8;
+	g_tx_cacheq_ptr = 0x3fcef844;
+	g_tx_done_cb_func = 0x3fcef8ac;
+	g_txop_queue_status_ptr = 0x3fcef8d8;
+	g_wifi_global_lock = 0x3fcef91c;
+	g_wifi_menuconfig_ptr = 0x3fcef908;
+	if_ctrl_ptr = 0x3fcef924;
+	lmacConfMib_ptr = 0x3fcef950;
+	mesh_rxcb = 0x3fcef83c;
+	net80211_funcs = 0x3fcef858;
+	our_tx_eb = 0x3fcef948;
+	our_wait_eb = 0x3fcef94c;
+	pTxRx = 0x3fcef954;
+	pp_sig_cnt_ptr = 0x3fcef930;
+	pp_task_hdl = 0x3fcef914;
+	pp_wdev_funcs = 0x3fcef944;
+	rc11BSchedTbl_ptr = 0x3fcef8fc;
+	rc11NSchedTbl_ptr = 0x3fcef8f8;
+	rcLoRaSchedTbl_ptr = 0x3fcef8f4;
+	s_encap_amsdu_func = 0x3fcef85c;
+	s_fragment_ptr = 0x3fcef928;
+	s_michael_mic_failure_cb = 0x3fcef8b4;
+	s_netstack_free = 0x3fcef840;
+	s_pp_task_create_sem = 0x3fcef910;
+	s_pp_task_del_sem = 0x3fcef90c;
+	s_wifi_queue = 0x3fcef918;
+	sta_rxcb = 0x3fcef838;
+	trc_ctl_ptr = 0x3fcef8ec;
+	wDevCtrl_ptr = 0x3fcef93c;
+	wDevMacSleep_ptr = 0x3fcef938;
+	wifi_sta_rx_probe_req = 0x3fcef8b0;
+	xphyQueue = 0x3fcef904;
+	/* ROM data, second block at 0x3ff1ee5x. */
+	g_wdev_last_desc_reset_ptr = 0x3ff1ee54;
+	our_controls_ptr = 0x3ff1ee50;
+	our_instances_ptr = 0x3ff1ee58;
+	/*
+	 * The coexistence ROM, group rom_coexist in esp32s3.rom.ld.
+	 *
+	 * libcoexist.a is linked in because esp_blob.c calls coex_pre_init()
+	 * and esp_coex_adapter_register() from its probe -- the two calls
+	 * IDF's own wifi_init.c makes from its SECONDARY system-init hook,
+	 * which this port has to make itself. The archive is a thin wrapper
+	 * over the ROM: nearly everything it references lives at a fixed
+	 * ROM address rather than in the archive, so the names have to be
+	 * bound here or the link fails on them.
+	 *
+	 * The six data pointers sit in the same internal-SRAM-1 block as the
+	 * Wi-Fi ROM data above (0x3fcef8xx, outside Linux's RAM). The two
+	 * functions are in the ROM's text window. All eight are copied
+	 * verbatim from esp32s3.rom.ld's rom_coexist group.
+	 *
+	 * coex_core_timer_idx_get is commented out in esp32s3.rom.ld itself
+	 * and is deliberately not bound here.
+	 */
+	coex_core_ble_conn_dyn_prio_get = 0x40005b8c;
+	esp_coex_rom_version_get = 0x40005b68;
+	coex_env_ptr = 0x3fcef834;
+	coex_pti_tab_ptr = 0x3fcef830;
+	coex_schm_env_ptr = 0x3fcef82c;
+	coexist_funcs = 0x3fcef828;
+	g_coa_funcs_p = 0x3fcef824;
+	g_coex_param_ptr = 0x3fcef820;
+
   . = KERNELOFFSET;
   /* .text section */
 
diff --git a/arch/xtensa/boot/dts/arduino-nano-esp32.dts b/arch/xtensa/boot/dts/arduino-nano-esp32.dts
index 1111111111111..2222222222222 100644
--- a/arch/xtensa/boot/dts/arduino-nano-esp32.dts
+++ b/arch/xtensa/boot/dts/arduino-nano-esp32.dts
@@ -37,6 +37,29 @@
 	};
 };
 
+/* The Wi-Fi blob port. This node is not a child of &ipc: nothing
+ * crosses to the other core, the Espressif libraries are linked into
+ * this kernel and run here. What the node carries is the one address
+ * the blob cannot find for itself -- the ROM's slot for the OS
+ * interface pointer, g_osi_funcs_p, at 0x3fcef940 in internal SRAM 1
+ * Block 8. That is outside Linux's RAM, which ends at 0x3e000000, so
+ * the driver ioremaps it and writes its own table there at probe.
+ *
+ * The address is a property rather than a constant in the driver so
+ * a different part number, or a ROM revision that moves the slot,
+ * needs a device-tree edit and not a rebuild.
+ *
+ * It hangs off &{/} rather than sitting at the top level: this file
+ * closes its root node above, and everything below is an overlay
+ * fragment, so a bare node here would be a syntax error.
+ */
+&{/} {
+	wifi_blob {
+		compatible = "esp,esp32-wifi-blob";
+		osi-funcs-address = <0x3fcef940>;
+	};
+};
+
 &iomux {
 	status = "ok";
 
diff --git a/drivers/net/wireless/espressif/esp32-ng/Kconfig b/drivers/net/wireless/espressif/esp32-ng/Kconfig
index 1111111111111..2222222222222 100644
--- a/drivers/net/wireless/espressif/esp32-ng/Kconfig
+++ b/drivers/net/wireless/espressif/esp32-ng/Kconfig
@@ -16,6 +16,15 @@
 config ESP32_WIFI_SHMEM
 	def_bool y
+	depends on !ESP32_WIFI_BLOB
 	select ESP32_IPC
 
+config ESP32_WIFI_BLOB
+	bool "Run the Wi-Fi blob inside Linux"
+	depends on ESP32_WIFI
+	help
+	  Link Espressif's closed-source Wi-Fi libraries into the kernel and
+	  host them directly, instead of talking to ESP-IDF firmware on the
+	  other core over shared memory.
+
 config ESP32_WIFI_SPI
 	bool
diff --git a/drivers/net/wireless/espressif/esp32-ng/Makefile b/drivers/net/wireless/espressif/esp32-ng/Makefile
index 1111111111111..2222222222222 100644
--- a/drivers/net/wireless/espressif/esp32-ng/Makefile
+++ b/drivers/net/wireless/espressif/esp32-ng/Makefile
@@ -1,5 +1,8 @@
 obj-$(CONFIG_ESP32_WIFI)		+= esp32-wifi.o
 esp32-wifi-objs				+= main.o esp_cmd.o esp_cfg80211.o esp_stats.o esp_utils.o
+esp32-wifi-$(CONFIG_ESP32_WIFI_BLOB)	+= esp_blob.o
+esp32-wifi-$(CONFIG_ESP32_WIFI_BLOB)	+= esp_blob_support.o
+esp32-wifi-$(CONFIG_ESP32_WIFI_BLOB)	+= esp_blob_phy.o
 esp32-wifi-$(CONFIG_ESP32_BT)		+= esp_bt.o
 esp32-wifi-$(CONFIG_ESP32_WIFI_SPI)	+= esp_spi.o
 esp32-wifi-$(CONFIG_ESP32_WIFI_SDIO)	+= esp_sdio.o
diff --git a/drivers/net/wireless/espressif/esp32-ng/esp_blob.c b/drivers/net/wireless/espressif/esp32-ng/esp_blob.c
new file mode 100644
index 0000000000000..1111111111111
--- /dev/null
+++ b/drivers/net/wireless/espressif/esp32-ng/esp_blob.c
@@ -0,0 +1,4304 @@
+// SPDX-License-Identifier: GPL-2.0-only
+/*
+ * The Espressif Wi-Fi blob, running inside Linux.
+ *
+ * This is the other half of the esp32-ng driver. The driver as it stands
+ * talks to ESP-IDF firmware on core 0 over shared memory: it sends commands
+ * and receives events, and the radio itself belongs to the other core. This
+ * file removes that core from the path. It links the same closed-source
+ * Wi-Fi libraries ESP-IDF links, supplies the OS interface they call
+ * through, and hands the driver an esp_if_ops backend that goes straight to
+ * the blob instead of over IPC.
+ *
+ * Why this is possible at all, measured rather than assumed:
+ *
+ *   - The blob's entire outside world is 18 symbols. Twelve of them are
+ *     mesh-only and disappear with libmesh.a; the remaining six are
+ *     WIFI_EVENT, ets_delay_us, hexstr2bin, puts and sprintf. Everything
+ *     else it references is either defined inside the blob set, defined by
+ *     the ROM linker scripts, or defined by libphy.a.
+ *
+ *   - The blob does not reference g_wifi_osi_funcs. Every one of its 37
+ *     objects that needs the OS references g_osi_funcs_p, a pointer the ROM
+ *     owns at 0x3fcef940. Installing the interface is therefore a single
+ *     store to a known address, not a function call.
+ *
+ *   - The blob exports esp_wifi_init_internal, not esp_wifi_init. The
+ *     public esp_wifi_init belongs to the IDF component that wraps it.
+ *
+ * There is no firmware handshake here and no IPC. The blob is the radio.
+ */
